WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.761
In this demonstration I follow one attack across two log sources,

2
00:00:04.761 --> 00:00:08.183
a firewall log and a Windows security event export,

3
00:00:08.183 --> 00:00:12.879
from the first failed logon to what the attacker did after getting in.

4
00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:19.119
The demo attack is a password spray, which is different from the brute force in Activity 4.3.

5
00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:22.044
First, the firewall log.

6
00:00:22.044 --> 00:00:27.518
Each line has the date and time, the firewall, the action, the protocol,

7
00:00:27.518 --> 00:00:32.459
the source and destination with ports, and the rule that matched.

8
00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:38.436
Who connected to the file server on port 445, SMB?

9
00:00:38.436 --> 00:00:46.824
I grep for colon 445, print field 6, the source, cut away the src equals and the port,

10
00:00:46.824 --> 00:00:50.433
then count with sort and uniq dash C.

11
00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:54.919
One address made ten connections: 10.10.30.25.

12
00:00:54.919 --> 00:01:01.552
That is in the branch VPN range, so someone came in through the VPN.

13
00:01:01.552 --> 00:01:08.671
The second line in the head output shows the VPN login from 203.0.113.88.

14
00:01:09.772 --> 00:01:11.607
Now the Windows events.

15
00:01:11.607 --> 00:01:18.871
awk with a comma separator keeps rows where field 3, the event ID, is 4625, a failed logon,

16
00:01:18.871 --> 00:01:21.425
and prints field 5, the account.

17
00:01:21.425 --> 00:01:27.172
Look at the pattern: nine different accounts, and each failed only once.

18
00:01:27.172 --> 00:01:31.164
Brute force is many passwords against one account.

19
00:01:31.164 --> 00:01:36.192
This is the opposite, one password tried against many accounts.

20
00:01:36.192 --> 00:01:41.859
That is a password spray, and it is designed to avoid account lockouts.

21
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.157
Did it work?

22
00:01:44.157 --> 00:01:49.147
I search for the success and the follow-up events.

23
00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:56.831
4624: svc_print logged on successfully from the same VPN address at 08:19:58.

24
00:01:56.831 --> 00:02:01.023
Then 5140, the Payroll share was accessed.

25
00:02:01.023 --> 00:02:10.104
Then 4688, schtasks was run, and 4698, a scheduled task called PrintSpoolCheck was created.

26
00:02:10.104 --> 00:02:16.391
The attacker got in, looked at payroll, and set up persistence.

27
00:02:17.492 --> 00:02:20.558
The same count works in PowerShell.

28
00:02:20.558 --> 00:02:27.218
Import-Csv, keep event ID 4625 with Where-Object, then Group-Object account.

29
00:02:27.218 --> 00:02:30.548
Nine groups, each with a count of one.

30
00:02:31.648 --> 00:02:33.639
Here are my findings.

31
00:02:33.639 --> 00:02:42.648
The attacker came in through the VPN from 203.0.113.88, sprayed nine accounts from 10.10.30.25,

32
00:02:42.648 --> 00:02:49.951
got in with svc_print, opened the Payroll share and created a scheduled task.

33
00:02:49.951 --> 00:02:55.072
That reaches the Installation phase of the Kill Chain.

34
00:02:55.072 --> 00:02:58.580
FILE-01 is compromised: escalate now.

35
00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:04.840
And notice, this matches alerts D-02 and D-08 from the first demo.

36
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:06.758
Your turn.

37
00:03:06.758 --> 00:03:11.178
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 4 and Activity 4.3.

38
00:03:11.178 --> 00:03:19.281
Use firewall dot log and windows underscore security underscore events dot csv in tilde slash day4.

39
00:03:19.281 --> 00:03:27.712
Find the attacking IP, count the failed attempts, find the successful logon and what happened after it.

40
00:03:27.712 --> 00:03:31.068
Is that pattern a spray or a brute force?

